// THE CLASSICAL AGE (480–323 BC) — English version of dados.js (same structure, same image ids).
// Dates follow the “middle chronology”; fifth-century dates rest mainly on Herodotus, Thucydides and Xenophon. Debated points are flagged. Army sizes and casualties are estimates.

const visao = [
  { caixa: 'In brief', texto: [
    'The **Classical period** (480–323 BC) is the century and a half in which ancient Greece reaches the height of its influence and of its violence. It opens with the Persian invasion of 480 BC, which the Greek cities defeat together (**Thermopylae**, **Salamis**, **Plataea**), and ends with the death of **Alexander the Great** in 323 BC. In between, **Athens** turns an alliance against Persia (the **Delian League**) into a maritime empire; under **Pericles** it builds the **Parthenon** and takes democracy to its most radical point; and it enters a war of nearly thirty years with **Sparta**, the **Peloponnesian War** (431–404 BC), from which it emerges defeated.',
    'Victory brought peace to no one. **Sparta** dominated Greece for a generation, until the **Thebes** of **Epaminondas** defeated it at **Leuctra** (371 BC) and freed Messenia; but the Theban hegemony was short too. On the edge of the Greek world a mountain kingdom was growing, **Macedon**, whose king **Philip II** beat the cities at **Chaeronea** (338 BC) and prepared the campaign against Persia that his son would carry out. It was also the time of Socrates and Plato, of Phidias, of Herodotus and Thucydides, of Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides: a huge part of what we call “classical culture” was born in these hundred and fifty years.'
  ] },
  { img: 'gcl-mapa-grecia-431', leg: 'Map of the Greek world in 431 BC, at the start of the Peloponnesian War, showing the areas of the Delian League (Athens) and the Peloponnesian League (Sparta).' },
  { h: 'Where and when' },
  'The setting is the **Aegean Sea** and its shores: mainland Greece (Attica, Boeotia, the Peloponnese, Thessaly, Macedon), the islands, the coast of Ionia, in today’s Turkey, and the western colonies, above all in Sicily and southern Italy. The opening and closing dates are conventions: 480 BC (Xerxes’ invasion) or 479 (Plataea) to begin, and 323 BC (Alexander’s death) to end. Some historians prefer to close in 338 BC (Chaeronea) or in 322 BC (the end of Athenian independence); the division between “Classical” and “Hellenistic” is, like all of them, an arrangement made afterwards.',
  { tabela: { cab: ['Phase', 'Dates', 'What marks it'], linhas: [
    ['The Persian Wars', '490 – 479 BC', 'Marathon (490), Thermopylae, Salamis and Plataea (480–479): victory over Persia'],
    ['Pentecontaetia: the Athens of Pericles', '478 – 431 BC', 'Delian League and Athenian empire; democratic reforms; the Parthenon; growing rivalry with Sparta'],
    ['The Peloponnesian War', '431 – 404 BC', 'Athens and Sparta, and their allies; plague; the Sicilian expedition; Athenian defeat at Aegospotami'],
    ['The Thirty and the democratic restoration', '404 – 403 BC', 'Oligarchic regime in Athens, civil war, amnesty'],
    ['Spartan hegemony', '404 – 371 BC', 'Sparta dominates the Aegean; Corinthian War; King’s Peace (387/6); the Cadmea'],
    ['Theban hegemony', '371 – 362 BC', 'Leuctra, liberation of Messenia, Mantinea'],
    ['The rise of Macedon', '359 – 323 BC', 'Philip II, Chaeronea (338), Alexander the Great; end of the poleis’ independence']
  ] } },
  { img: 'gcl-mapa-liga-delos', leg: 'Map of the members of the Delian League (the Athenian empire) and of the Peloponnesian League, around 431 BC.' },
  { h: 'What changed in 480 BC' },
  'Until 480 BC, Greece was a collection of independent cities, often at war with each other and, on the fringes, under the shadow of the **Persian Empire**. Persia had conquered the cities of Ionia around 546 BC; their revolt (499–494 BC), with brief support from Athens, led to two invasions: that of 490 BC, stopped at **Marathon**, and that of 480–479 BC, much bigger, led by King **Xerxes** himself. In 481 BC, representatives of dozens of cities met at the Isthmus of Corinth and joined in a league, commanded by Sparta on land and, in large part, by Athens at sea.',
  'It was the first time that a large number of Greek cities acted together, and the victory gave them a new confidence: the idea that Greeks, free and governed by laws, were different from the “barbarians” subject to a king. The Athenians themselves made that victory the centre of their self-image; that is why much of what was written afterwards, even by critical authors, reads in the light of 480.',
  { img: 'gcl-partenon-oeste', leg: 'The Parthenon seen from the west, on top of the Acropolis of Athens. Built between 447 and 432 BC, it is the best-known symbol of Classical Athens.' },
  { h: 'The main players' },
  { lista: [
    '**Athens:** radical democracy, naval power, cultural centre; between 478 and 404 BC it dominates the Aegean through the Delian League.',
    '**Sparta:** a barracks-city, with helots working the land; a land power and leader of the Peloponnesian League. It wins the Peloponnesian War but loses its hegemony at Leuctra.',
    '**Thebes:** a city of Boeotia which, after siding with the Persians in 480, became the leading Greek power between 371 and 362 BC.',
    '**Corinth, Argos, Megara, Syracuse:** cities of medium or large weight, almost always on a collision course with the greatest; Syracuse, in Sicily, is the largest Greek state of the West.',
    '**Macedon:** a monarchy of the north, with language and customs close to Greek (a debated question), which becomes dominant under Philip II.',
    '**Persia:** the Great King is the enemy until 479 BC, but afterwards he pays and arbitrates: Persian money decides the Peloponnesian War and the Corinthian War.'
  ] },
  { h: 'What sources we have' },
  'The Classical period is the best-documented in Greek history, but the documentation is uneven:',
  { lista: [
    '**Herodotus** (c. 484 – c. 425 BC): the *Histories*, about the Persian Wars. He gives many details and legends, and his numbers are exaggerated; he is the only continuous source.',
    '**Thucydides** (c. 460 – c. 400 BC): the *History of the Peloponnesian War*, down to 411 BC; a contemporary, rigorous, but he selects and simplifies.',
    '**Xenophon** (c. 430 – c. 354 BC): the *Hellenica*, which continue Thucydides to 362 BC, and the *Anabasis*; he is pro-Spartan and omits important things (for instance, the founding of Messene).',
    '**Aeschylus and other poets:** *The Persians* (472 BC) is the only theatrical witness to a recent historical event.',
    '**Orators:** Lysias, Isocrates, Aeschines and Demosthenes give us everyday Athens, with partiality.',
    '**Inscriptions:** decrees, tribute lists, public building accounts (such as those of the Erechtheion), funerary stelae. This is the source that most obliges us to correct the historians.',
    '**Plutarch** (c. 46 – after AD 119) and **Diodorus of Sicily** (1st century BC) wrote centuries later, and use sources that are lost; they are indispensable, but must be read with caution.'
  ] },
  { caixa: 'Chronology: a note of caution', texto: 'The Greeks had no common era: each city counted years by its magistrates (in Athens, the eponymous archon) and had its own calendar, with the year starting in summer. That is why many dates are given with a slash (508/7 BC, 404/3 BC): they mean that the event took place somewhere in the Athenian year running from one summer to the next. Some very well-known dates (the Peace of Callias, the exact date of Salamis, that of Chaeronea) are debated, and the soldier counts given by Herodotus, in particular, should not be taken literally.' },
  { img: 'gcl-atenas-pericles', leg: 'Artist’s reconstruction of Athens in the time of Pericles, around 430 BC, with the Acropolis, the city, the Long Walls and the port of Piraeus in the background; details and colours are hypothetical. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { h: 'Why they matter' },
  { lista: [
    '**Democracy and its limits:** in Athens the idea that citizens govern was invented and argued over (in assemblies, in courts, in the theatre), and it was soon seen that democracy can also err, condemn Socrates and vote for disastrous wars.',
    '**Political thought:** Thucydides’ history, the plays, the thought of Plato and Aristotle were born of concrete crises of these decades.',
    '**A war that reads like an essay:** Thucydides’ account of the Peloponnesian War became a classic analysis of power, war and decline.',
    '**Art and architecture:** the Parthenon, the sculpture of Phidias, Polykleitos and Praxiteles, and the theatre fixed what Europe understood by “classical”.',
    '**A model and a warning:** the wars between Greek cities show how rivalry between neighbours makes life easy for a third party (Persia, then Macedon).'
  ] },
  { caixa: 'The Classical age today', texto: 'The **Acropolis of Athens** has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1987, and the **Acropolis Museum**, opened in 2009, holds the Parthenon sculptures that remained in Athens. Marathon, Thermopylae and Salamis are places one can visit; the **Monument to Leonidas** at Thermopylae dates from 1955. The modern *marathon* was born in 1896, at the first Olympic Games of the modern era, in Athens, to evoke the run from Marathon; it is a late legend, but it stuck.' },
  { h: 'Beware of myths' },
  'This period is full of popular images that history does not confirm or confirms only in part: the “300 Spartans” who fought alone at Thermopylae; Pericles as ruler of a “golden age” without critics; the “egalitarian” Sparta or the Athens “free for all”; the millions of Persian soldiers; Socrates saying “I know that I know nothing”. Each section below flags what is fact, what is tradition and what is probability or modern reconstruction.'
];

const linha = [
  'This timeline follows the main events from 480 to 323 BC. Dates are given in the “middle chronology”; those of the fifth century rest mainly on Herodotus and Thucydides, those of the fourth on Xenophon, Diodorus and inscriptions. In the most debated cases, this is indicated.',
  { linha: [
    { d: '481 – summer 480 BC', t: 'Thermopylae and Artemisium', x: 'In the autumn of 481 BC, delegates from Greek cities met at the **Isthmus of Corinth** and formed a league against Persia, with Sparta in command. In 480, when **Xerxes**’ army enters Greece, the plan is to stop it at the pass of **Thermopylae**, while the fleet faces the Persian one off **Artemisium**, in Euboea. The Spartan king **Leonidas** defends the pass for three days with some 5,000 to 7,000 men (the numbers vary between sources); on the third day, after a local Greek, **Ephialtes**, shows the Persians a path over the mountain, Leonidas sends most men away and stays with the 300 Spartiates, about 700 Thespians and about 400 Thebans (the latter, according to Herodotus, surrender). The fleet, meanwhile, withdraws from Artemisium after combats with no clear winner.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'gcl-termopilas-combate', leg: 'Imagined scene of the last stand at Thermopylae, 480 BC, with Spartan and Thespian hoplites against the Persians in the pass; representative equipment, hypothetical details. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'September 480 BC', t: 'Athens evacuated; Salamis', x: 'With the way open, the Persians enter **Attica**; the Athenians had evacuated the population to Troezen, Salamis and Aegina, and the city is sacked and the **Acropolis** burned. The Greek fleet, with some 310 to 380 triremes (sources differ), gathers in the strait of **Salamis**. **Themistocles**, according to Herodotus, persuades the commanders to fight there, in a narrow space, and sends a deceptive message to Xerxes that leads him to attack. The Persians, more numerous, lose order among their ships and are defeated. Xerxes withdraws; the general **Mardonius** stays in Greece with part of the army.' },
    { d: '479 BC', t: 'Plataea and Mycale', x: 'In the summer of 479, an allied army of perhaps 30,000 to 40,000 hoplites, plus light troops, under **Pausanias**, regent of Sparta, defeats Mardonius at **Plataea**, in Boeotia; Mardonius dies in the fighting. According to tradition, in the same period the Greek fleet defeats the Persians at **Mycale**, in Ionia. The invasion is over, and the Greeks go on the offensive.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'gcl-plateias-batalha', leg: 'Imagined scene of the battle of Plataea, 479 BC, with Greek hoplites in formation against the Persians; hypothetical details. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '478/7 BC', t: 'The Delian League', x: 'After Pausanias offended many allies with his authoritarian behaviour at Byzantium, Sparta gives up the command of the war against Persia. Athens takes it over: the **Delian League** is created, an alliance of cities of the islands and Asia Minor, with its seat and treasury on Delos. According to Thucydides, **Aristides** sets the initial contribution at 460 talents a year; many members pay in money, instead of supplying ships, and Athens keeps the fleet.' },
    { d: 'c. 466 BC', t: 'The Eurymedon', x: 'The Athenian general **Cimon** destroys a Persian fleet and army beside the river **Eurymedon**, in Pamphylia (southern Turkey today). The date, traditionally c. 466, is sometimes given as 469. Naxos, which had tried to leave the League, had been forced to stay; Thasos will do the same and be besieged (465–463 BC): the League is becoming an empire.' },
    { d: '464 – 462 BC', t: 'The Spartan earthquake and the break with Athens', x: 'A strong earthquake strikes **Sparta** (c. 464 BC) and the **helots** of Messenia revolt. Sparta asks Athens for help; **Cimon** brings a contingent, but is sent back, which offends the Athenians. It is the beginning of the break between the two powers.' },
    { d: '462/1 BC', t: 'Ephialtes and radical democracy', x: '**Ephialtes** strips the **Areopagus**, the council of former magistrates, of almost all the political and judicial powers it still had, passing them to the Assembly, the Council of 500 and the people’s courts. Ephialtes is assassinated soon after; **Cimon**, who opposed him, is ostracized in 461. The young **Pericles** is rising.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'gcl-mapa-guerras-medicas', leg: 'Map of the second Persian War, 480–479 BC, with the Persian advance, Thermopylae, Artemisium, Salamis and Plataea.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 460 – 446 BC', t: 'The “First Peloponnesian War”', x: 'Athens fights Corinth, Aegina, Megara and, at times, Sparta. The **Long Walls** linking the city to Piraeus are built (c. 461–456). In 457, Sparta wins at **Tanagra**, but Athens wins soon after at **Oenophyta** and controls Boeotia for a decade. A great expedition to **Egypt** (c. 460–454 BC), in support of a revolt against Persia, ends in disaster, with the loss of many ships. With Boeotia lost (Coronea, 447), the **Thirty Years’ Peace** (446/5 BC) recognizes the division of the Greek world between the two leagues.' },
    { d: '449 BC', t: 'The Peace of Callias', x: 'According to later sources, around 449 BC Athens and Persia end the war by a treaty negotiated by **Callias**: Persia recognizes the freedom of the cities of Ionia and keeps its ships out of the Aegean. Thucydides does not mention it, and the **existence of the treaty is debated** by historians; some understanding probably existed, of uncertain form and content.' },
    { d: '447 – 432 BC', t: 'The Parthenon and the Athens of Pericles', x: 'Athens is at its height. The League’s treasury, which had been moved from Delos to Athens c. 454 BC, pays, among other things, for the new building programme on the **Acropolis**: the **Parthenon** (447–432, architects Ictinus and Callicrates, with the sculpture under the direction of **Phidias**), the **Propylaea** (437–432), and later the **Erechtheion** (c. 421–406). In 451/0, a law of Pericles restricts citizenship to the children of an Athenian father **and** mother.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'gcl-oracao-funebre', leg: 'Imagined scene of Pericles delivering the funeral oration for the dead of the first year of war, in the winter of 431/0 BC, in the public cemetery of the Kerameikos; the words we know are Thucydides’, and the setting is hypothetical. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '431 BC', t: 'The Peloponnesian War begins', x: 'In the spring of 431, **Thebes**, an ally of Sparta, tries to seize **Plataea**, an ally of Athens. Sparta, with its League, declares war, and King **Archidamus II** invades Attica. **Pericles**’ strategy: bring the population of Attica behind the Long Walls, let the Spartans ravage the fields and use the fleet to attack enemy coasts. At the end of the first year, Pericles gives the **funeral oration**, known through Thucydides’ version.' },
    { d: '430 – 426 BC', t: 'The plague of Athens', x: 'With the whole of Attica crowded inside the walls, an epidemic breaks out that kills a huge part of the population, perhaps a quarter or more, and **Pericles** himself (429). Thucydides, who fell ill and survived, described the symptoms; the agent of the disease has never been identified with certainty.' },
    { d: '425 – 421 BC', t: 'Pylos, Amphipolis and the Peace of Nicias', x: 'In 425, the general **Demosthenes** fortifies **Pylos**, in Messenia, and the Athenians capture 292 hoplites (120 of them Spartiates) who had been cut off on the island of **Sphacteria**: a shock for Sparta. In 422, at **Amphipolis**, the two most warlike leaders die, the Athenian **Cleon** and the Spartan **Brasidas**. The **Peace of Nicias** (421 BC) was meant to last fifty years; it lasted little.' }
  ] },
  { linha: [
    { d: '415 – 413 BC', t: 'The Sicilian expedition', x: 'Athens sends a great armada (134 triremes at departure, according to Thucydides) to subdue **Syracuse**, at the request of a Sicilian city and at the instigation of **Alcibiades**. He is summoned back to Athens to be tried for impiety (the case of the mutilated **Herms** and the Mysteries), flees to Sparta and advises it. **Nicias**, who stayed in command, sees the Syracusans, helped by Sparta, blockade and destroy the fleet and the army; Nicias and Demosthenes are executed, and thousands of prisoners (Thucydides speaks of about 7,000) are shut in the quarries of Syracuse.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'gcl-expedicao-siciliana', leg: 'Imagined scene of the Athenian armada in the Great Harbour of Syracuse, 415–413 BC; hypothetical reconstruction, with no claim to exactness. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { img: 'gcl-latomias-siracusa', leg: 'The quarries (latomiai) of Syracuse, in Sicily. According to Thucydides, the Athenians captured in 413 BC were held in quarries of the city; the exact location is uncertain.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '413 – 406 BC', t: 'Decelea, the Four Hundred and Arginusae', x: 'Sparta occupies and fortifies **Decelea**, in Attica, which deprives Athens of its fields and causes thousands of slaves to flee. With Persian money, it builds a fleet. In 411 BC, an oligarchic coup installs the government of the **Four Hundred** in Athens; it falls after some months, while the Athenian fleet at **Samos** remains democratic and recalls **Alcibiades**. In 406, Athens wins at **Arginusae**, but a storm prevents the rescue of the shipwrecked, and six victorious generals are condemned and executed in a collective trial that later seems unlawful.' },
    { d: '405 – 404 BC', t: 'Aegospotami and the surrender of Athens', x: 'In 405, the Spartan admiral **Lysander**, with money from Cyrus the Younger, surprises the Athenian fleet on the beach at **Aegospotami**, in the Hellespont, and captures almost all the ships (about 170); the roughly 3,000 prisoners are executed. Besieged and starving, **Athens** surrenders in the spring of 404: the Long Walls are demolished, the fleet handed over (only twelve ships are left) and the city joins the Spartan alliance. Corinth and Thebes ask for the city to be destroyed; Sparta refuses.' }
  ] },
  { linha: [
    { d: '404 – 403 BC', t: 'The Thirty Tyrants and the restoration', x: 'With Spartan support, thirty oligarchs, among them **Critias**, govern Athens for eight months and kill, according to Aristotle, about 1,500 people. **Theramenes**, who opposed the excesses, is condemned to death. **Thrasybulus** and the democratic exiles seize the fort of Phyle, then Piraeus; Critias dies in battle. With Spartan mediation, the **amnesty of 403/2** is agreed: it has few parallels in ancient history, and democracy is restored.' },
    { d: '401 – 399 BC', t: 'Cyrus, the Ten Thousand and Socrates', x: 'In 401, **Cyrus the Younger** tries to take the throne from his brother Artaxerxes II; some 10,000 Greek mercenaries fight at his side at **Cunaxa**, in Mesopotamia. With Cyrus dead, the Greeks retreat across northern Persia to the Black Sea, under leaders who include **Xenophon**, who told it in the *Anabasis*. In 399, in Athens, **Socrates** is tried for impiety and for corrupting the young, and condemned to death by a jury of 501 citizens.' }
  ] },
  { linha: [
    { d: '395 – 386 BC', t: 'The Corinthian War and the King’s Peace', x: 'Sparta, after dominating Greece, faces a coalition of Thebes, Athens, Corinth and Argos, with Persian money. In 394, the Persian fleet commanded by the Athenian **Conon** destroys the Spartan fleet at **Cnidus**. The war ends with the **King’s Peace**, or Peace of Antalcidas (387/6 BC), dictated by the Persian king: the Greek cities of Asia go to Persia, and the rest are declared “autonomous”. Sparta becomes guardian of the peace.' },
    { d: '382 – 377 BC', t: 'The Cadmea and the Second Athenian League', x: 'In 382, a Spartan general occupies the **Cadmea**, the citadel of Thebes, in time of peace. In 379/8, a group of Theban exiles liberates the city, with **Pelopidas** at their head. In 378/7, Athens founds a new maritime alliance, the **Second Athenian League**, which guarantees members that they will pay no tribute and receive no garrisons; according to Diodorus, it brings together about 70 cities.' }
  ] },
  { linha: [
    { d: '6 July 371 BC', t: 'Leuctra', x: 'In Boeotia, the Spartan army (perhaps 10,000 men) is defeated by about 7,000 Thebans and allies. **Epaminondas** placed the best hoplites on the left wing, about fifty ranks deep, and made the decisive attack there. King **Cleombrotus** dies, and, according to Xenophon, about 400 of the roughly 700 Spartiates present fall. Sparta, with fewer and fewer full citizens, will never recover its former strength.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'gcl-leuctra-batalha', leg: 'Imagined scene of the battle of Leuctra, 371 BC, with the deep Theban column, in which the Sacred Band fought, attacking the Spartan right wing; hypothetical details. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '370 – 362 BC', t: 'Epaminondas in the Peloponnese and Mantinea', x: 'Epaminondas invades **Laconia**, the heart of Spartan territory, where, according to tradition, no enemy army had entered for centuries (the city, without walls, is not taken). He frees **Messenia** (c. 370/69), where **Messene** is founded on Mount Ithome, and supports the founding of **Megalopolis**, capital of the Arcadians. In 362 BC, at **Mantinea**, the Thebans win, but Epaminondas dies of his wound. Xenophon ends the *Hellenica* there, noting that Greece was left even more confused than before.' },
    { d: '359 – 346 BC', t: 'Philip II and the Third Sacred War', x: '**Philip II** becomes king of Macedon in 359 BC, reforms the army and takes **Amphipolis** (357). The **Social War** (357–355) breaks up the Second Athenian League. In the **Third Sacred War** (356–346), Phocis occupies Delphi and uses the sanctuary’s treasure; Philip is called in to fight the Phocians and defeats them at the **Crocus Field** (c. 353/2). In 349/8 he destroys **Olynthus**, and **Demosthenes** makes his speeches against him in Athens (the *Philippics* and the *Olynthiacs*). The **Peace of Philocrates** (346) gives Philip a seat on the council of the Delphic sanctuary.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'gcl-queroneia-batalha', leg: 'Imagined scene of the battle of Chaeronea, 338 BC, with the Macedonian sarissa phalanx advancing against the Theban and Athenian line; hypothetical details. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { img: 'gcl-leao-queroneia', leg: 'The Lion of Chaeronea, a 4th-century BC monument over the collective tomb of the Thebans killed in the battle of 338 BC (traditional identification).' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '338 – 336 BC', t: 'Chaeronea and the League of Corinth', x: 'In the summer of 338 BC, at **Chaeronea**, in Boeotia, Philip’s army, with his son **Alexander** (18) commanding a wing of the cavalry, defeats Athens, Thebes and their allies. The Theban **Sacred Band** dies almost to a man. Philip treats Athens moderately, but puts a garrison in Thebes. In 337 BC he gathers the Greek cities at Corinth (except Sparta) in the **League of Corinth**, which chooses him as leader of a war against Persia. He is assassinated in 336 at Aegae, during his daughter’s wedding.' },
    { d: '336 – 322 BC', t: 'Alexander and the end of the Classical age', x: 'Alexander destroys **Thebes** in 335 BC after a revolt, and sets out against Persia the following year. He dies in Babylon in June 323. When the news reaches Greece, Athens leads the **Lamian War** (323–322) against Macedon, which it loses at Crannon; **Demosthenes** kills himself, **Aristotle** dies at Chalcis, and Athens accepts a Macedonian garrison and restrictions on democracy. It is the date that some historians choose for the definitive end of the poleis’ independence.' }
  ] }
];

